ic events emitted twice."},"2060":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2061":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","type":"page","content":"Layer F: Ledger level","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f"},"2062":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Every hazard in layer F of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2063":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Upgrade ledgers, empty and very large ledgers, parallel Soroban stages, change order that varies between core processes, and the same ledger in different bytes."},"2064":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2065":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","type":"page","content":"Layer G: Protocol and network variants","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Every hazard in layer G of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2067":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Bytes that depend on the network passphrase, older protocols, mainnet-only code paths in core, and CAPs gated before mainnet."},"2068":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2069":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","type":"page","content":"Layer H: Reader robustness (SDK-only)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Every hazard in layer H of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2071":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Depth bombs, over-length vectors, truncated input and non-canonical encodings that an SDK must reject safely."},"2072":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","type":"page","content":"Concepts","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/concepts"},"2074":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-0","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"The handful of words the corpus uses: entry, variant, hazard, placement, fact, evidence, focus, change order."},"2075":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-1","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Entry** | One rare shape. A directory under `entries/` with an `entry.json` and one XDR file per variant, plus up to two alternate-ordering files when the change order varies. Its `id` is permanent and never reused. |","table":"table-0"},"2076":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-2","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Catalog file** | The hand-written source of an entry, `catalog/<id>.json`: its intent and facts, addressed relative to the focus transaction. `make build` (`tools/build.mjs`) compiles it into the entry. |","table":"table-0"},"2077":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-3","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                 |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Family** | The first part of an entry id, such as `failure`, `signer` or `archival`. The docs group entries by it. |","table":"table-0"},"2078":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-4","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Variant** | One ledger file of an entry, for one protocol, one network passphrase and one emitter-flag profile. Its key reads like `p30-mainnet-generated`; a `-galexie` or `-bare` suffix names a non-base profile. |","table":"table-0"},"2079":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-5","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Flag profile** | Which of stellar-core's six metadata flags a run sets: `base` (the shipped variants), `galexie` (all six, as public data lakes are written) or `bare` (none). The ledger is the same; the metadata is not. |","table":"table-0"},"2080":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-6","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Lane** | How a variant was made: `generated` by a standalone stellar-core, `mined` from ledger history, or `synthetic` (hand-built; none yet). The recipes are also split into two lanes, classic and Soroban. |","table":"table-0"},"2081":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-7","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Hazard** | A named way readers break, with its evidence, from `caps/registry.json`. For example, `H-A3` is a signed-payload signer stored as 33 bytes. |","table":"table-0"},"2082":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-8","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Placement** | Where in the XDR a hazard sits in this entry. For example, `SetOptionsOp.signer.key`. |","table":"table-0"},"2083":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-9","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                 |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Fact** | A value a correct reader must find at a JSON Pointer path in the SEP-0051 rendering, as `equals`, `length` or `some` (a matching change, in any order). |","table":"table-0"},"2084":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-10","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word         | Meaning                                                                      |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Evidence** | What backs a fact: `construction`, `snapshot`, `core`, `walker` or `review`. |","table":"table-0"},"2085":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-11","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **SDK check** | A rule for an SDK helper rather than a value, such as \"return all 5 entries or raise an error\". |","table":"table-0"},"2086":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-12","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                               |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Focus** | The transaction (and operation) the entry is about, so a reader knows where to look in a full ledger. |","table":"table-0"},"2087":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-13","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Change order** | Whether stellar-core always emits the operation's changes in the same order. When it doesn't, the entry pins an order-normalised hash and lists every ordering seen. |","table":"table-0"},"2088":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-14","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Channel** | `stable`, `next` (gated CAPs before mainnet) or `embargoed` (never committed publicly). All entries today are `stable`. |","table":"table-0"},"2089":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"page","content":"How it works","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-0","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"Recipes drive a bare stellar-core to emit rare shapes; the bytes become entries with facts; independent readers check them, real indexers ingest them, and SDK builds read the same bytes."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-1","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"1. Make the shapes"},"2092":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-2","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"2. Keep the bytes, write down the truth"},"2093":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-3","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"3. Check the truth independently"},"2094":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-4","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"4. Run it against real code"},"2095":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-5","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"A **recipe** is a deterministic script that drives one fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 over its HTTP port. The core image is pinned by digest. There is no RPC, no network, and no keys or funds: seeds and close times are fixed. Core writes every closed ledger as `LedgerCloseMeta`."},"2096":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-6","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"Some shapes only happen on a real network, or are worth having as real network bytes, so they are **mined** from public ledger history instead. `mined/` holds  real mainnet and testnet ledgers;  entries are built from  of them, the testnet ledger where wallet-backend halted among them."},"2097":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-7","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"Each interesting ledger becomes a corpus **entry**, compiled from a hand-written catalog file. An entry holds:"},"2098":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-8","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the raw bytes;"},"2099":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-9","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the CAP and the hazard it targets;"},"2100":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-10","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"where in the XDR the hazard sits;"},"2101":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-11","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the **facts** a correct reader must find, each with what backs it."},"2102":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-12","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                | Reader                                                                         | Target        |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| Byte-for-byte round trip, every fact | native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0; the WASM build must agree wherever it can decode | `make verify` |","table":"table-0"},"2103":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-13","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                    | Reader                                                           | Target        |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| Fact values also read by | stellar-core's own `dump-xdr` (C++) and XDRProof's schema walker | `make oracle` |","table":"table-0"},"2104":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-14","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                        | Reader                                         | Target          |\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| Which union arms are reached | a Python walker over the v30.0 XDR definitions | `make coverage` |","table":"table-0"},"2105":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-15","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                                                                  | Reader                             | Target                 |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| Every arm reached by an entry, or classified with the core source line that decides it | the schema walker, per transaction | `make coverage-report` |","table":"table-0"},"2106":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-16","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                 | Reader                                                                  | Target         |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| No entry is redundant | leave-one-out over each entry's arms, hazards, sightings and placements | `make novelty` |","table":"table-0"},"2107":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-17","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against  | How                                                                                                                                        | Target             |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------ |\n| The SDKs | builds of the JavaScript, WASM, Rust, Go and Python SDKs read every corpus variant, plus strkey, invalid-input, depth and sighting vectors | `make conformance` |","table":"table-1"},"2108":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-18","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against    | How                                                                                                          | Target        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| An indexer | real wallet-backend builds ingest every corpus ledger, one isolated session each, through a mock stellar-rpc | `make replay` |","table":"table-1"},"2109":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-19","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"The figure also shows an indexer linter, a GitHub Action and the hardening guide, all marked planned. None of these exist yet; today the checks run as `make` targets."},"2110":{"id":"/docs/start","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"page","content":"What XDRProof is","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start"},"2111":{"id":"/docs/start-0","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"A shared corpus of rare but valid Stellar ledger data, the facts a correct reader must find in it, and tools that replay it into indexers and test SDKs on the same shapes."},"2112":{"id":"/docs/start-1","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"What is in it"},"2113":{"id":"/docs/start-2","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"Check every claim yourself"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/start-3","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"An indexer has run fine for months. Then a ledger arrives with a shape its code has never seen:"},"2115":{"id":"/docs/start-4","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a signer that carries a payload;"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/start-5","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a trustline created by a contract call, with no event;"},"2117":{"id":"/docs/start-6","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a map whose keys all read as \"1\"."},"2118":{"id":"/docs/start-7","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"Ingestion halts, or worse, it keeps going and stores something wrong. The team fixes it. Next month another team, in another language, meets the same shape."},"2119":{"id":"/docs/start-8","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**XDRProof turns each of those shapes into a test everyone can run.** One team's incident becomes every team's regression test, and one SDK's fix becomes a check on all of them."},"2120":{"id":"/docs/start-9","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part       | What it is                                                                                                     | Where      |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Corpus** | entries of real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes, each tagged with its CAP, with the facts a correct reader must return | `entries/` |","table":"table-0"},"2121":{"id":"/docs/start-10","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                | What it is                                                   | Where                |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- |\n| **Hazard registry** | named ways readers break, in  layers, each with its evidence | `caps/registry.json` |","table":"table-0"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/start-11","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                    | What it is                                                           | Where       |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| **Schema and manifest** | JSON Schema 2020-12 for an entry; a generated index of every variant | `schema/v1` |","table":"table-0"},"2123":{"id":"/docs/start-12","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part              | What it is                                                                                                                                    | Where    |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| **Mined ledgers** | 37 real mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, for shapes only a real network produces, or worth having as real network bytes | `mined/` |","table":"table-0"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/start-13","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part        | What it is                                                                              | Where      |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Recipes** | Transactions for a bare standalone stellar-core that regenerate every generated variant | `recipes/` |","table":"table-0"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/start-14","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part      | What it is                                                                                   | Where    |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| **Tools** | Verify, oracle, coverage, generate, order scan, conformance and replay, each a `make` target | `tools/` |","table":"table-0"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/start-15","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"The numbers on these pages come from the repository's own data or from a `make` target you can run, and each page names which. Start with Quickstart."},"2127":{"id":"/docs/start-16","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"The RFP also asks for an indexer **linter** and an **indexer-hardening guide**, and XDRProof will ship as a **GitHub Action** and packages. None of these exist in the repository yet. The registry already marks the hazards the linter and the guide will cover."},"2128":{"id":"/docs/start-17","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"This repository backs a proposal to the Stellar Community Fund's RFP *XDR/CAP-Coverage Audit Tool and Indexer-Hardening Guide* (SCF #46). The corpus grows entry by entry from here. XDRProof is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by the Stellar Development Foundation."},"2129":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart"},"2130":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-0","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"Clone the repository and run make verify: strkey vectors, every entry against its schema, hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip and every fact, and every mined ledger, in seconds."},"2131":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-1","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#get-the-code","content":"Get the code"},"2132":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-2","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"Check what you have"},"2133":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-3","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"Verify the corpus"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-4","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Go further"},"2135":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-5","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `make verify` | Node ≥ 22.15 and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-6","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target          | Needs                                                                         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage` | Python 3 and Node ≥ 22.15 (`coverage-goldens` also needs git and the network) |","table":"table-0"},"2137":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-7","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                               | Needs                              |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage-report`, `make novelty`, `make build` | Node ≥ 22.15, npm and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2138":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-8","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                     |\n| ------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `make oracle` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm |","table":"table-0"},"2139":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-9","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                                    | Needs                                     |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `make generate`, `make generate-quick`, `make order-scan` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq, perl |","table":"table-0"},"2140":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-10","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target             | Needs              |\n| ------------------ | ------------------ |\n| `make conformance` | Node, Rust, Docker |","table":"table-0"},"2141":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-11","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                                                              |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `make replay` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git; the network on the first run only |","table":"table-0"},"2142":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-12","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"The first run installs the tool dependencies (`npm ci`) and builds the native reader (`cargo build --release`), about a minute. Then it checks:"},"2143":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-13","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every SEP-0023 strkey test vector;"},"2144":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-14","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every entry against the schema, and its hazards against the registry;"},"2145":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-15","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"size and sha256;"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-16","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"a decode and re-encode through **native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0** that must reproduce identical bytes;"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-17","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the focus transaction is in the ledger;"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-18","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every expected fact;"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-19","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json`, wherever it can decode, renders the same JSON;"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-20","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that `manifest.json` is current;"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-21","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every mined ledger and its findings."},"2152":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-22","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Each target has its own page under Tools. `make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance and replay."},"2153":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"page","content":"Conformance","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance"},"2154":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"make conformance: every corpus variant, the strkey vectors, invalid XDR and a depth ladder through builds of the JavaScript, Go, Rust and Python SDKs. Each build's known failures are written down first; anything else fails the run."},"2155":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"The builds"},"2156":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"What each family checks"},"2157":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"The recorded matrix"},"2158":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Every known failure, and why"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"How deep each SDK reads"},"2160":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"Needs Node ≥ 22.15, Rust and Docker. Every SDK build answers the same questions about the same bytes, and the run prints one matrix: which SDK reads which ledger correctly."},"2161":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Each build runs as a **testee**: a small process that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and answers one JSON line on stdout. The runner (`conformance/run.mjs`) judges every answer against a reference and against that build's pre-registered expectations."},"2162":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee      | Package                                                                                     | Families |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `js@17.4.0` | `@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0 (its XDR layer is `@stellar/js-xdr` 5, written in JavaScript) | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2163":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee            | Package                                                             | Families              |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `xdr-json@30.0.0` | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM | corpus, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2164":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee        | Package                                          | Families                      |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `rust@30.0.0` | `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 with `stellar-strkey` 1.0.0 | all but the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2165":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee      | Package                                    | Families                                        |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `go@v0.9.0` | `github.com/stellar/go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0 | all but SEP-0051 JSON and the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2166":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee          | Package                                                     | Families |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `python@17.0.1` | `stellar-sdk` 17.0.1 (py-stellar-base), the current release | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2167":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee             | Package                                                                                                | Families                      |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n| `python-xdr@0.2.1` | `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 and `stellar-strkey` 0.2.0, which py-stellar-base's next major release is built on | corpus, strkey, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2168":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee                                                              | Package                                      | Families  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `js@17.2.0` · `js@17.2.1` · `js@17.3.0` · `go@v0.7.1` · `go@v0.7.2` | the releases before and after upstream fixes | sightings |","table":"table-0"},"2169":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Java, Swift, Dart, PHP and .NET SDKs can join by implementing the same protocol: `hello`, `decode`, `encode_json`, `strkey_decode` and `native`. A testee answers `unsupported` for anything its SDK cannot do, and that is recorded as not applicable, not as a failure."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                        | Passes when                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `corpus` | every variant of every entry | it decodes; the re-encoding is byte-identical; where the SDK renders SEP-0051 JSON, the JSON equals rs-stellar-xdr's and every expected fact of the entry holds on it |","table":"table-1"},"2171":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                         | Passes when                                                                |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `strkey` | the SEP-0023 test vectors, plus a deterministic sample of every kind of address in the corpus | valid keys decode to the right kind and payload; invalid keys are rejected |","table":"table-1"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Passes when                                                      |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `lane-s` | hand-built invalid XDR: truncated, trailing bytes, a bool of 2, unknown and negative discriminants, a symbol past its maximum, non-zero padding, an optional flag of 2, a huge length with no data, a path longer than five | every invalid input is rejected; the valid controls are accepted |","table":"table-1"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family  | Input                                     | Passes when                                                                |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `depth` | an `ScVal` vector nested 64 to 4,000 deep | measured: the deepest value each SDK decodes, and whether anything crashes |","table":"table-1"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                              | Passes when                                                                                           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sdk-check` | each entry's `expected.sdk_checks` | the helper behaves as the check says, for example a native projection keeps every map entry or raises |","table":"table-1"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                                | Passes when                                                            |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sightings` | `conformance/vectors/sightings.json` | the upstream fix behaves as fixed; the releases before it show the bug |","table":"table-1"},"2176":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"This is `conformance/recorded/matrix.md` from the last run. **known** means a failure that is written down in that build's expectations, with its cause."},"2177":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"`js@17.4.0` and `python-xdr@0.2.1` read every corpus variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does: the bytes, the SEP-0051 JSON and every expected fact."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Each build's `conformance/expectations/<testee>.json` lists every check it is known to fail, with the reason and the finding it belongs to. They are written from a measured run and reviewed by hand. After that the run must match them exactly: **an unexpected failure and an unexpected pass both fail it**, so a new bug and an upstream fix both show up."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"Nothing crashes on the depth ladder; each SDK refuses cleanly past its limit. The deepest sampled depth each one decodes:"},"2180":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"A depth limit counts XDR nesting, and one `ScVal` level costs several units, so equal limits don't mean equal reach: the WASM decoder and the Python packages both use 500 and differ fourfold. The corpus has ledgers stellar-core applied whose Soroban authorization tree is 245 levels deep, and the WASM decoder refuses them (finding F-1 on Findings)."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"page","content":"Coverage","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage"},"2182":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"make coverage and make coverage-report: every XDR union arm reachable from LedgerCloseMeta is either reached by a corpus entry or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it."},"2183":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Every arm accounted for"},"2184":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"A walker reads the rs-stellar-xdr definitions for v30.0, and lists every union arm and enum member reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta`. It then walks real ledgers and records which arms they meet."},"2185":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                          | Union arms               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` | **** (and  enum members) |","table":"table-0"},"2186":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                                                           | Union arms |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entry files ( variant files: raw `.xdr`, alternate change orderings, and `.xdr.zst` decompressed) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                    | Union arms |\n| -------------------------- | ---------- |\n| The mined ledgers ( files) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2188":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                            | Union arms |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entries and mined ledgers together | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                               | Union arms |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| stellar-core's goldens (866 files, 15,306 ledgers, commit `f2b91947`) | 254 of 499 |","table":"table-0"},"2190":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"The corpus rows are computed from the corpus at commit , the same way `make coverage` computes them. The goldens row is a fixed measurement at stellar-core commit `f2b91947`. The goldens are the ledgers stellar-core's own tests produce."},"2191":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"`make coverage` counts every arm in an entry's file, including arms from other transactions that happen to share its ledger. `make coverage-report` is stricter. An entry **reaches** an arm only when its own transaction (envelope, fee changes, apply meta) or its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys) contains it. Every arm no entry reaches is classified in `coverage/exclusions.json`, with the stellar-core source line that decides it. The run fails if an arm is in neither list, or in both."},"2192":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Enum members reached by an entry:  of . The report is written to `COVERAGE.md`, with the entries that target each hazard."},"2193":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"No `todo` arm is left: every arm stellar-core can still write at protocol 30 on a standalone network is in an entry. Only the  `network-only` arms, which need a live network, are out of reach. A closed protocol-30 ledger never carries a `validation-only` or `not-emitted` arm, and a `historical` one appears only in ledgers of older protocols."},"2194":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"page","content":"Generate","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"make generate: 20 fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 runs, in pairs, over both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair must agree, and every shipped generated variant must come out again."},"2196":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"The matrix"},"2197":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#what-must-hold","content":"What must hold"},"2198":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"Each pair agrees"},"2199":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"Every shipped variant is reproduced"},"2200":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The profiles are compared"},"2201":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"It needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq and perl, and host ports 21700–21719. The stellar-core image is pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and every run refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0."},"2202":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"`make generate` runs **10 pairs**, which is 20 fresh standalone cores, 6 at a time. There is no RPC, no network, and no keys or funds."},"2203":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2204":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2206":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P28      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2207":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2208":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"The two runs of a pair are compared ledger by ledger. They must be byte-identical, or identical after normalising. Normalising sorts the order of changes within an operation and zeroes the diagnostic `invoke_time_nsecs` timing value."},"2209":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"The list of shipped generated variants is read from `entries/` (`tools/shipped-checks.mjs`), so the testnet and protocol-28 variants are re-checked too. A full run also fails if any shipped variant was not assigned to a pair."},"2210":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The same recipe ledgers under the three flag profiles are compared, and the result is recorded in `recipes/<lane>/profiles/p30-mainnet.json`. Every ledger keeps the same header hash; only the metadata changes. See Found while building."},"2211":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"It ends with `OK: every run reproduced; every shipped generated variant reproduced`."},"2212":{"id":"/docs/tools","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"page","content":"Tools","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/tools-0","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"Every check is a make target: verify, oracle, coverage, novelty, generate, order scan, conformance and replay. Each one re-runs a claim and prints its result."},"2214":{"id":"/docs/tools-1","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"The readers behind them"},"2215":{"id":"/docs/tools-2","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Needs              | Time    |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make verify` | Strkey vectors hold. Every entry matches the schema, sizes and hashes match, and every variant round-trips byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0. The focus transaction is present, every fact holds, the WASM reader renders the same JSON wherever it can decode, and the manifest is current. Every mined ledger is verified | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2216":{"id":"/docs/tools-3","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                          | Needs                     | Time    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------- |\n| `make oracle` | Each fact's values are also read by stellar-core's own decoder and by the schema walker | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2217":{"id":"/docs/tools-4","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                  | Needs                  | Time    |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------- |\n| `make coverage` | union arms are reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` at v30.0; the entries reach , the mined ledgers | Python 3, Node ≥ 22.15 | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2218":{"id":"/docs/tools-5","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                 | What it proves                                                                                                                                                              | Needs              | Time    |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make coverage-report` | Every one of the  arms is reached by an entry's own transaction or ledger shape () or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it (). Writes `COVERAGE.md` | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/tools-6","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target         | What it proves                                                                                        | Needs              | Time    |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make novelty` | No entry is redundant: leave-one-out over  atoms (arms, enum members, hazards, sightings, placements) | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2220":{"id":"/docs/tools-7","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                  | What it proves                                                  | Needs                  | Time        |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------- |\n| `make coverage-goldens` | stellar-core's own 866 golden ledger files reach 254 of the 499 | Python 3, git, network | about 1 min |","table":"table-0"},"2221":{"id":"/docs/tools-8","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                      | Needs                             | Time         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| `make generate` | 20 fresh standalone cores, in pairs, across both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair agrees, and every shipped generated variant is reproduced | Docker, Node, npm, curl, jq, perl | about 10 min |","table":"table-0"},"2222":{"id":"/docs/tools-9","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                | What it proves                      | Needs    | Time        |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------- | ----------- |\n| `make generate-quick` | The two mainnet base P30 pairs only | the same | about 3 min |","table":"table-0"},"2223":{"id":"/docs/tools-10","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target            | What it proves                                                                 | Needs  | Time         |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------ | ------------ |\n| `make order-scan` | 20 fresh runs per lane: which ledgers vary in change order; content never does | Docker | about 15 min |","table":"table-0"},"2224":{"id":"/docs/tools-11","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target             | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | Needs              | Time             |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------- |\n| `make conformance` | Every corpus variant, the strkeys, the invalid-input vectors and a depth ladder go through  SDK builds. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0 and `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 read every variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does; every other difference is pinned with its cause | Node, Rust, Docker | about 2 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2225":{"id":"/docs/tools-12","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                 | Needs                           | Time              |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `make replay` | The whole corpus into wallet-backend `main` ( of  handled,  halted), and every fixed failure before and after its fix. Every verdict is pinned | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git | about 20 min cold |","table":"table-0"},"2226":{"id":"/docs/tools-13","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance and replay."},"2227":{"id":"/docs/tools-14","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make build` is not a check: it compiles `catalog/` into `entries/` from the runs of `make generate` and `mined/`, in minutes. See Catalog."},"2228":{"id":"/docs/tools-15","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Native reader | A small Rust program on `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 (depth limit 1500) that decodes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and re-encodes | verify, novelty, coverage-report, conformance (the reference), replay |","table":"table-1"},"2229":{"id":"/docs/tools-16","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader      | What it is                                           | Used by             |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| WASM reader | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, a second opinion | verify, conformance |","table":"table-1"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/tools-17","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader       | What it is                                                                | Used by |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| stellar-core | Core's own C++ `dump-xdr`, in Docker with no network, on the pinned image | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2231":{"id":"/docs/tools-18","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- |\n| Schema walker | `walker.mjs`: XDRProof's own JavaScript reader, driven only by the XDR definitions; it shares no code with any SDK | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2232":{"id":"/docs/tools-19","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader          | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by  |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------- |\n| Coverage walker | `xdrcov.py`: a Python walker over the same v30.0 definitions that records every union arm and enum member it meets | coverage |","table":"table-1"},"2233":{"id":"/docs/tools-20","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader         | What it is                                                                                                                             | Used by                  |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Segment walker | the schema walker again, attributing every arm it meets to the transaction envelope or result meta it sits in, or to the ledger itself | coverage-report, novelty |","table":"table-1"},"2234":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"page","content":"Novelty","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty"},"2235":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"make novelty: leave-one-out over every entry's arms, enum members, hazards, sightings and placements. The run fails if any entry holds nothing another entry doesn't."},"2236":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"Today"},"2237":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"The corpus grows by purpose, not volume. Novelty checks that every entry earns its place. It describes each entry as a set of **atoms**:"},"2238":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom                        | What counts                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Union arms and enum members | those reached by the entry's own transaction (its envelope and result meta) or by its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys), in any variant. Other transactions that share the ledger count for nothing |","table":"table-0"},"2239":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom    | What counts                   |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Hazards | each hazard the entry targets |","table":"table-0"},"2240":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom      | What counts                       |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Sightings | each upstream issue it reproduces |","table":"table-0"},"2241":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom       | What counts                                        |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Placements | each pair of a hazard and where in the XDR it sits |","table":"table-0"},"2242":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"An entry is **redundant** when every one of its atoms is also held by some other entry. Each entry is left out in turn, so the result does not depend on the order entries are read, unlike a greedy \"does it add anything new\" pass. The run exits 1 if any entry is redundant."},"2243":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"At commit :  redundant entries, over  atoms."},"2244":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                           | Count |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| Hold an arm, enum member, hazard or sighting no other entry holds |       |","table":"table-1"},"2245":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                        | Count |\n| ------------------------------ | ----- |\n| Told apart only by a placement |       |","table":"table-1"},"2246":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                        | Count |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| … of which every other atom sits inside one single other entry |       |","table":"table-1"},"2247":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"<Callout title=\"Placement is a claim, not a measurement\">A placement is text the author wrote. So `make novelty` lists every entry told apart only by placement, and flags the ones wholly contained in one other entry: for each such pair, the facts must check a different thing.</Callout>"},"2248":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"It needs Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (Rust), which finds each variant's transaction hashes."},"2249":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"page","content":"Oracle","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle"},"2250":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"make oracle: each fact's values must also appear in what stellar-core's own C++ decoder reads and in what XDRProof's schema walker reads. No SDK is involved."},"2251":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A planted error must be caught"},"2252":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"A fact is only as good as the reader that produced it. The oracle asks two readers that share no code with any SDK:"},"2253":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader           | How it runs                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **stellar-core** | `stellar-core dump-xdr`, inside `docker run --network none` on the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image` (override with `XDRPROOF_CORE_IMAGE`) |","table":"table-0"},"2254":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader            | How it runs                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Schema walker** | `tools/walker.mjs`, driven only by the v30.0 XDR definitions. It rejects non-zero padding, over-length values, bad enums and trailing bytes |","table":"table-0"},"2255":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"For each fact, the oracle takes its expected values and checks that they appear among the values each reader produced. Enum values are checked too: `tools/coverage/defs/sep0051-enum-names.json` maps every XDR enum member to the string SEP-0051 prints for it, so the walker confirms a member it reached and stellar-core one its dump printed. Enum names are matched by name across all enums, anywhere in the ledger, so a common name such as `success` is a weak confirmation. A `length` fact has no value to check, and a `v0`-style arm name is structure, not a value. A value either reader misses is a **disagreement**, and the run fails. So does a ledger stellar-core cannot print: nothing in it is confirmed. With `--write`, it records `core` and `walker` in the fact's evidence."},"2256":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A reader that finds every value might simply accept anything. So the oracle tests itself. It takes every expected value of 10 or more digits (64-bit amounts, IDs and fees), changes its last digit and asks both readers again."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                | Counts as |\n| ------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| Both readers reject the changed value | caught    |","table":"table-1"},"2258":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                                                      | Counts as                    |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Both readers find it: the changed value occurs elsewhere in the same ledger | a coincidence, not a failure |","table":"table-1"},"2259":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                   | Counts as                         |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------- |\n| Only one reader finds it | a **disagreement**: the run fails |","table":"table-1"},"2260":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"If no planted error is caught at all, the self-test fails and the run exits 1."},"2261":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"<Callout title=\"What it does not check\">It checks that a value is present in a reader's output, not that it sits at the fact's path; the path is checked by Verify.</Callout>"},"2262":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"page","content":"Order scan","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan"},"2263":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"make order-scan: 20 fresh runs per recipe lane record which ledgers come out in more than one byte order, and prove the content never varies."},"2264":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The committed results"},"2265":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"stellar-core iterates a hash map with a per-process random salt, so the changes within an operation can come out in a different order on every run. The order scan measures this. It runs each recipe lane 20 times on fresh cores, records every byte ordering seen per ledger, and fails if any ledger's **content** differs once order is normalised."},"2266":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Results are in `recipes/classic/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json` and `recipes/soroban/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json`:"},"2267":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order               | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Classic |      | of  recipe ledgers;  differ in every run | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | -------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Soroban |      | of  recipe ledgers         | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2269":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The protocol-upgrade ledger (2) varies in both lanes as well; it is never an entry."},"2270":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Corpus entries whose order varies pin an order-normalised hash, and list every raw ordering seen (`change_order` in the schema)."},"2271":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"},"2272":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"page","content":"Replay","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay"},"2273":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"make replay: every corpus ledger into a real indexer through a mock stellar-rpc, one fresh store per ledger. Each variant gets a verdict from the indexer's own state: its cursor, its process and what it stored. Never from logs."},"2274":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"wallet-backend main, the whole corpus"},"2275":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Every fix, before and after"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"A session"},"2277":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"Derive the probes"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"Serve the ledger"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"Prepare, start and watch"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Observe"},"2281":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"Verdicts"},"2282":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Probes"},"2283":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"Bring your own indexer"},"2284":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"Needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (`make native`). Once the images and Go modules are cached nothing reaches the network: the ledgers come from the corpus. On a laptop under Docker, six sessions at a time, the full run takes about 9.5 minutes once the builds are cached."},"2285":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"The reference indexer is stellar/wallet-backend. At `main` () it ingests  variants in  sessions: ** handled,  halted**. The other  are each pinned with their cause in `replay/expectations/wallet-backend@main.json`:"},"2286":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix (`replay/sightings.json`). Each must fail before and pass at the fix."},"2287":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"One session replays one ledger, one distinct variant (its bytes and its network), into a fresh store."},"2288":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"`replay/lib/derive.mjs` reads the ledger with rs-stellar-xdr and lists the **probes**: the facts a correct indexer has stored after ingesting it. They come from the ledger alone."},"2289":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"`replay/lib/mock-rpc.mjs` serves the ledger as stellar-rpc v30.0.1 would: `getLedgers`, `getHealth`, `getLatestLedger`, `getNetwork`, `getVersionInfo`, and `getLedgerEntries` from the state the ledger leaves behind. Pagination, page limits and validation errors follow stellar-rpc word for word."},"2290":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"The adapter gives the indexer a clean store whose cursor sits just before the ledger and starts it against the mock. The harness polls the cursor until it passes the ledger, the process exits, the cursor stops moving, or the attempt times out. Once the ledger is passed, the process must still be running a second later. If the ledger was not passed, the indexer is started once more on the same store."},"2291":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"The adapter answers every probe from what the indexer stored, and the harness compares."},"2292":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Before any session counts, the harness replays the **controls**: the plainest ledger of each protocol the target can decode. Each must get past its ledger, or the run stops as a setup failure."},"2293":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `handled` | the cursor passed the ledger and every applicable probe matches |","table":"table-0"},"2294":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                          |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dropped` | the cursor passed, and a fact the ledger states is not stored |","table":"table-0"},"2295":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict      | Rule                                                                                 |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `mishandled` | the cursor passed, and a stored value differs from the ledger, or an invariant fails |","table":"table-0"},"2296":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                                                             |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `stalled` | the first attempt stopped making progress on the ledger without exiting; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2297":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `crashed` | the first attempt exited on the ledger; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2298":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict  | Rule                              |\n| -------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `halted` | neither attempt passed the ledger |","table":"table-0"},"2299":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict       | Rule                                                                                    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `unsupported` | the ledger's protocol is newer than the target decodes, or its network is not supported |","table":"table-0"},"2300":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict | Rule                                                         |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `error` | an adapter command failed; never a finding (the run exits 2) |","table":"table-0"},"2301":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Each probe names a fact by its key and states the expected value in a canonical form: strkeys, decimal strings, XDR enum names. A muxed `M…` account is named by its `G…` account."},"2302":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-29","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                                         | Expected                                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.present`, `tx.result`, `tx.fee_charged`, `tx.operations`, `tx.inner_hash` | the transaction is stored, with its result code, fee charged, operation count and, for a fee bump, the inner hash |","table":"table-1"},"2303":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-30","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                              | Expected                                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.participant`, `op.participant` | the source and fee source are attributed to the transaction; each operation's source to its operation |","table":"table-1"},"2304":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-31","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                    | Expected                                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `account.created`, `account.removed`, `contract.created` | the accounts and contract instances the transaction created or removed are recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-32","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                              | Expected                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `signer.added`, `signer.updated`, `signer.removed` | the signer change is recorded, with its new weight |","table":"table-1"},"2306":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-33","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                  | Expected                                                     |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `trustline.added`, `trustline.removed` | the trustline the transaction created or removed is recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2307":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-34","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                 | Expected                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `balance.native`, `balance.trustline` | the balance at the end of the ledger, where the ledger changes it |","table":"table-1"},"2308":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-35","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Two **invariants** cover everything the indexer stored in the session: `addresses.valid` (every stored address decodes and is one the ledger names) and `facts.extra` (every stored creation, merge and trustline change is one the ledger states). Facts on which indexers differ on purpose are not asserted."},"2309":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-36","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The indexer is reached only through an **adapter**: a directory under `replay/adapters/` with an `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands (`prepare`, `start` and `cursor` are required; `observe`, `setup`, `stop`, `finish` and `teardown` are optional). Any indexer that reads `getLedgers` can be tested the same way. An adapter that implements only the three required commands still gets `handled`, `crashed` and `halted` for every ledger. The full contract is in replay/README.md."},"2310":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-37","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The wallet-backend adapter builds each commit in a pinned Go image, runs TimescaleDB 2.28.2 as its compose file does, copies each session's database from a migrated template, runs `wallet-backend ingest` in live mode against the mock, and answers probes with one read of the session database."},"2311":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-38","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"<Callout title=\"A new failure, a changed one and a fixed one all fail the run\">`replay/expectations/<adapter>@<target>.json` lists every variant that is not `handled`, with the exact probes that failed, the cause and the upstream issue. It also pins the set of variants judged (their count and a sha256 of their ids), so none can drop out of a run unnoticed.</Callout>"},"2312":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"page","content":"Verify","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify"},"2313":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"make verify: strkey test vectors, then every entry against the schema, its hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact; then every mined ledger."},"2314":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#what-it-checks","content":"What it checks"},"2315":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Strkeys"},"2316":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry"},"2317":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"The bytes and the round trip"},"2318":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus and the facts"},"2319":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"A second reader"},"2320":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-manifest","content":"The manifest"},"2321":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-mined-ledgers","content":"The mined ledgers"},"2322":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-create-contract-authorization-payload","content":"The create-contract authorization payload"},"2323":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#output","content":"Output"},"2324":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Every SEP-0023 test vector must decode and re-encode (valid), or be rejected (invalid). This covers XDRProof's own strkey codec, which the schema walker uses."},"2325":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry is validated against `schema/v1/entry.schema.json` (ajv, strict mode). Its `id` must match its directory, and every hazard id must exist in the registry. Variant keys must be unique, and each file name must start with its key."},"2326":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"Size and sha256 must match; `.zst` variants are checked on their decompressed bytes. The **native reader** (rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0, depth limit 1500) decodes the bytes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and encodes them again. The result must be identical, byte for byte."},"2327":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus transaction hash must be in the ledger, as an outer or fee-bump inner hash. Every fact that applies to the variant must hold at its JSON Pointer path. If the entry's change order is unstable, the order-normalised hash must match, and the shipped bytes must be one of the orderings seen."},"2328":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"The WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0 decodes each variant again, with no fallback. If it renders different JSON, the run fails. If it cannot decode a variant at all (for example at its depth limit), verify lists it and records `fails` in the manifest, and 