ching, liquidity pools, claimable balances, and fees that differ from what was bid."],[1902,"Files"],[2015,"Filing F-1 and F-3 is the maintainers' call. Issues that would let anyone halt or crash an indexer are handled under the disclosure policy (`findings/policy.md`) and are not listed here."],[2255,"For each fact, the oracle takes its expected values and checks that they appear among the values each reader produced. Enum values are checked too: `tools/coverage/defs/sep0051-enum-names.json` maps every XDR enum member to the string SEP-0051 prints for it, so the walker confirms a member it reached and stellar-core one its dump printed. Enum names are matched by name across all enums, anywhere in the ledger, so a common name such as `success` is a weak confirmation. A `length` fact has no value to check, and a `v0`-style arm name is structure, not a value. A value either reader misses is a **disagreement**, and the run fails. So does a ledger stellar-core cannot print: nothing in it is confirmed. With `--write`, it records `core` and `walker` in the fact's evidence."],[2331,"For the two v1 create-contract entries, `tools/auth-payload-check.mjs` encodes each authorization preimage by hand twice, once as the entry's own bytes and once as the V2 rendering the host hashes, and verifies the ed25519 signature against both. The entry stellar-core accepted verifies only as V2; the one signed over its own bytes verifies only as v1, and core failed it. No SDK or host code is involved. See hazard H-B16."],[1973,"Found while building"],[907,"Four path payments in a fee bump: two succeed through a liquidity pool, then NO_TRUST and SRC_NO_TRUST"],[1741,"Four trustlines from three operation sources in one transaction, one alphanum-12 at the int64-max limit"],[1453,"Galexie's default flags include ENABLE_SOROBAN_DIAGNOSTIC_EVENTS."],[2194,"Generate"],[2131,"Get the code"],[2134,"Go further"],[93,"HOST_FUNCTION_TYPE_CREATE_CONTRACT authorized by address credentials, root SOROBAN_AUT